Data: 7463 insured-corn county-years (2013-2024, 631 counties), 630 high-hazard (NASA POWER hazard_z > 1), mostly 2021-2023; 2012 cannot be used with a lagged 5-year CDL window (CDL starts 2008). Primary treatment share_3plus (share of cropland in 3+ crop rotations, window ending t-1): only 11% of its variance is within county (within SD 0.053 vs between 0.153). Across counties it tracks crop mix and geography (Spearman with corn share -0.70, irrigation +0.20, longitude -0.32, mean corn drought loss cost +0.51). Effect on corn drought loss cost in high-hazard years per +10 percentage points of cropland in 3+ crop rotations: county FE + state-year FE OLS +12.6% (95% CI +4.6 to +20.5); PPML +4.4% (95% CI -9.7 to +20.7); pooled with controls +11.6% (95% CI +5.2 to +18.1). Negative = protective. Secondary treatments (FE OLS): n_crops_5y per +0.1 crops +0.2% (95% CI -4.3 to +4.7); Shannon per +0.1 -14.5% (95% CI -24.4 to -4.7), but Shannon PPML -1.6% (95% CI -17.9 to +17.9). Diagnostics (primary): low-hazard placebo passes; excess-moisture placebo FAILS; lead test passes; robust to a confounder as strong as hazard x irrigation: yes (robustness value 0.041). Shannon: lead test FAILS, excess-moisture placebo FAILS, robust to irrigation-strength confounder: no. Adding 2012 via the contemporaneous Shannon window (includes the crop planted in year t, before the summer hazard): +32.2% (95% CI +20.9 to +43.4) per +0.1 Shannon. Verdict: NO robust observational effect. The primary 'how many crops' measure is wrong-signed (more 3+ crop rotation, more corn drought loss) within counties. The Shannon index does look protective in the linear FE model, but it does not survive the proportional (PPML) model, the lead test, the excess-moisture placebo or a confounder as strong as irrigation. It also flips to a harmful sign when 2012 is added through the contemporaneous window. County-year insurance data with little within-county change in rotations cannot confirm (or rule out) the field-trial effect. The app therefore shows the Bowles et al. (2020) trial range (14-90% smaller drought-year maize yield losses) as a labelled scenario assumption, with the low end (no effect) included in its range.
